The scenario is finished in `env/env_0034_senior_living_operator/` and the whole-scenario check passes: `validate_scenario` returns VALID with 0 errors and 0 warnings. That covers 5 difficulties, 10 of 10 tasks loading, 56 tools and 333 KB documents.

**What was built.** The operator is Larkspur Senior Living, with seven product categories. Five are tested, each with 2 tasks:

| Difficulty | Product | Task audit | KB audit | KB docs |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| independent_living_billing_and_move_out | independent | PASS (round 3) | PASS (round 1) | 60 |
| assisted_living_care_levels | assisted | PASS (round 3) | PASS (round 2) | 64 |
| respite_and_trial_stays | respite | PASS (round 4) | PASS (round 1) | 62 |
| entrance_fee_contracts_and_refunds | entrance | PASS (round 2) | PASS (round 1) | 59 |
| personal_services_packages | services | PASS (round 4) | PASS (round 3) | 49 |

Memory care (21 docs) and dining (18 docs) are KB-only products that no task tests. They passed their document checks but were never audited, which is expected for products no task tests.

After the last changes I re-ran every check myself, not just the agents. All five difficulties pass all of their own checks. All seven products pass the document check. No phone number, SSN last four or email appears in more than one difficulty.

**Things you should know:**
- **Six problems were found and fixed in later rounds:**
  - Every difficulty except entrance had at least one task fail its first audit; all were fixed through rework and re-audit.
  - One task's knowledge-base example copied that task's own account records, so the documents were re-rendered with new examples.
  - The respite tools used Python's `datetime`, which the runtime forbids. Only the final whole-scenario check caught it. It was fixed with identical behaviour and re-audited.
  - The services KB audit traced its failures back to the services policy rules themselves, so those rules were fixed first. Services task 0's correct answer changed from a 3-month to a 4-month suspension, and the documents were then re-rendered and re-audited.
- **Only the first audit round of each difficulty was fully blind.** Later rounds resumed the same auditor, who had already seen the answers; each report says so. A fresh agent could re-check the reworked material blind if you want that.
- **Many business rules are invented.** Discounts, tiers and phase-ins were added by the agents and are declared as extensions to the base charter, so the audits checked them for consistency, not real-world accuracy. They were judged plausible, and the clearly unrealistic ones were removed, such as tax-letter percentages tied to a resident's own history. They still deserve a domain read.
- **Minor observations remain open** in the audit reports. Each auditor confirmed none of them changes any task's answer.
- **One isolation slip:** a search by the services KB agent briefly listed file names from other scenarios. It reported using none of their content, and I logged it.
- **Agents ran in batches.** Here, agents launched together all return together, so I advanced each difficulty to its next stage batch by batch rather than the moment it finished. This cost time, not correctness.
- **Interruption:** the assisted-living agent was cut off when the account switched. I found its agent ID in the session transcript and resumed it from where it stopped.

The full history (each agent run, round and result) is in `env/env_0034_senior_living_operator/worklog.md`. The audit reports and the final validation output are under `env/env_0034_senior_living_operator/audits/`. Nothing is committed; the folder is untracked on `main`.
